/-
  Certificates for the mismatches between the Rev. Procs. (2022-38, 2023-34, 2024-40, 2025-32) and the statute.
  The item-by-item table is ITEMS.tsv (generated by tools/arith/findings_table.py); this header is the summary.

  COUNTS (published figures recomputed from the statute; every one a `decide` theorem)
    RP 2024-40 (2025): 181 checked — 142 match, 4 match up to non-statutory rounding, 27 penalties match only under
      the IRS's one-year-lag reading (F2), 8 mismatch (F1).
    RP 2025-32 (2026 + re-stated 2025 items): 194 — 123 match, 4 rounding, 57 penalties lag reading (34 of them
      literally not computable, F9), 10 mismatch (F1, F3).
    RP 2023-34 (2024, subset): 73 — 64 match, 7 lag, 2 mismatch (F1).  RP 2022-38 (2023, subset): 73 — 63 match,
      7 lag, 3 mismatch (F1, F6).
    Structural checks (tax columns, MFS = 1/2 MFJ, phase-out ends, EIC derived amounts): 46 — 43 hold, 3 fail (F5).
    SSA base + COLA 2025/2026: 4/4.  Retirement (§415(d) method 11 amounts, §1(f) 10 amounts) + HSA 4, 2025/2026:
      50/50.  §36B: 2025 table = statutory (iii) table; 2026 not checkable (F10).
    Pub. 15-T 2025/2026: 12 annual tables and 48 wage-bracket tables, all structurally exact (Withholding.lean).
    Not checkable: §36B(f)(2)(B) caps, §148 regulatory amounts, Rev. Proc. 90-12 / 98-19 / 2002-41 amounts,
      the IRS's long-term-care index (F4), daily wage-bracket tables.

  F1 (high). The IRS rounds the adjusted AMOUNT where the statute rounds the INCREASE; when the base is not a
     multiple of the unit the statutory result keeps the base's remainder, so the published figures are impossible
     for any index value (theorems below): §63(c)(7)(B)(ii) HoH standard deduction 2026 $24,150 (statute $24,175);
     §6334(d)(4)(C) wage-levy amount and §642(b)(2)(C)(iii) trust exemption 2023–2026 $4,700/$5,000/$5,100/$5,300
     (statute $4,750/$5,050/$5,150/$5,250); §7702B(d) per diem $420/$430 (statute, IRS index: $425/$435) and the
     §213(d)(10) $375 tier $900/$930 ($905/$935).
  F2 (medium). Penalties (§§6651(j), 6652(c)(7), 6695(h), 6698(e), 6699(e), 6721(f), 6722(f)) use the COLA of the
     year before the one the statute names ("the calendar year" of filing): all 98 reproduce under that reading.
     Literal amounts are higher, e.g. filed 2026: §6721(a)(1) cap $4,098,500 vs $4,191,500, §6651 $525 vs $535;
     filed 2024: §6721 $310 vs $330 per return.
  F3 (medium-high). 2026 estates' first bracket $3,300 = base 2016; §1(j)(3)(B)(i) (Pub. L. 119-21) keeps 2017 for
     amounts where a bracket above 22% begins → $3,250.
  F4. §213(d)(10)(B)(ii) as written (medical C-CPI-U vs medical CPI × ALL-ITEMS C/P(2016)) gives far lower limits;
     the IRS figures are a CPI-U-medical → C-CPI-U-medical splice at Aug 2016 with amount rounding (proved).
  F5 (medium). MFS maximum 15% capital-gain amount is $25 below 1/2 of the joint amount in 2023, 2024, 2025.
  F6 (medium-high). 2023 qualifying-relative limit $4,700; the IRS's own method (as for 2024–2026) gives $4,800;
     $4,700 is the §6334(d)/§642 figure. (Literally §151(d)(5)(A) makes it zero.)
  F7. Text errors: RP 2023-34 §3.53 "filed in 2024" (should be 2025); RP 2025-32 §4.57–4.58 "August 1, 2026"
     (should be 2027); RP 2023-34 §3.01 "excess over $191,150" (should be $191,950) in three tables; RP 2024-40
     §2.12 / 2025-32 §4.11 publish a §59(j) cap although §55(d)(4)(A)(iii) disables §59(j) after 2017.
  F8. Drafting: §45R(d)(3)(B)(ii) "multiplied by the cost-of-living adjustment"; §877(a)(2) stale "1992 ...
     subparagraph (B)"; §213(d)(10)(B)(ii) (F4); Notice 2025-67 n.1 itself flags §45E(f)(2)(C)(iii).
  F9. October 2025 C-CPI-U was never published: the statutory 2026 average (all 2027 adjustments) is incomplete.
  F10 (low). §36B 2026: one factor in [1.0479, 1.0483] fits all 12 cells; HHS's own 2013→2025 factor
     1.0060718427 would give 9.56% not 9.96%; 2025 RCP 9.02% vs HHS factor → 8.65%. Inputs not published.
  Vintage: with today's revised C-CPI-U, 231 items would differ; every match uses the §1(f)(6)(A) vintage.
-/
import TaxCode.Indexing.RP24_40
import TaxCode.Indexing.RP25_32
import TaxCode.Indexing.RP23_34
import TaxCode.Indexing.RP22_38
namespace TaxCode.Indexing.Findings
open TaxCode.Indexing

/-! ## 1. The IRS rounds the adjusted amount where the statute rounds the increase

When a base amount is not a multiple of the rounding unit, a rounded *increase* keeps the base's remainder, so some
published figures cannot be produced by the statutory rule with any C-CPI-U values at all. -/

/-- §63(c)(7)(B): the $23,625 head-of-household amount plus an increase rounded down to $50 always ends in 25 or 75;
    Rev. Proc. 2025-32 §4.14(1) publishes $24,150 for 2026. -/
theorem sd_hoh_never_24150 (num den : Nat) : adjustND 23625 num den (.incDown 50) ≠ 24150 := by
  intro h; have := incDown_mod 23625 num den 50; rw [h] at this; contradiction

/-- §6334(d)(4)(C) (and §642(b)(2)(C)(iii), which adopts it): $4,150 plus an increase rounded down to $100 always
    ends in 50; the Rev. Procs. publish $5,100 (2025) and $5,300 (2026). -/
theorem levy_never_round_hundreds (num den : Nat) :
    adjustND 4150 num den (.incDown 100) ≠ 5100 ∧ adjustND 4150 num den (.incDown 100) ≠ 5300 := by
  have := incDown_mod 4150 num den 100
  constructor <;> intro h <;> rw [h] at this <;> contradiction

/-- The same for the 2023 and 2024 figures ($4,700 and $5,000 in Rev. Procs. 2022-38 and 2023-34). -/
theorem levy_never_round_hundreds_2023_2024 (num den : Nat) :
    adjustND 4150 num den (.incDown 100) ≠ 4700 ∧ adjustND 4150 num den (.incDown 100) ≠ 5000 := by
  have := incDown_mod 4150 num den 100
  constructor <;> intro h <;> rw [h] at this <;> contradiction

/-- §213(d)(10)(B)(i) (and §7702B(d)(5), "in the same manner"): increases are rounded to the nearest $10, so the
    $175 per-diem and the $375 premium tier always end in 5. Published: $420/$430 and $900/$930. -/
theorem ltc_never_round_tens (num den : Nat) :
    adjustND 175 num den (.incNear 10) ≠ 420 ∧ adjustND 175 num den (.incNear 10) ≠ 430 ∧
    adjustND 375 num den (.incNear 10) ≠ 900 ∧ adjustND 375 num den (.incNear 10) ≠ 930 := by
  have h1 := incNear_mod 175 num den 10
  have h2 := incNear_mod 375 num den 10
  refine ⟨?_, ?_, ?_, ?_⟩ <;> intro h
  · rw [h] at h1; contradiction
  · rw [h] at h1; contradiction
  · rw [h] at h2; contradiction
  · rw [h] at h2; contradiction

/-- With the statute's index, rounding the AMOUNT instead reproduces the published figures. -/
theorem amount_rounding_reproduces :
    (let q := ratio ccpiu_v2025 cpiu ccpiu_med_v2025 cpiu_med (.year 2024) 2026; adjustND 23625 q.1 q.2 (.amtDown 50)) = 24150 ∧
    (let q := ratio ccpiu_v2024 cpiu ccpiu_med_v2024 cpiu_med (.year 2017) 2025; adjustND 4150 q.1 q.2 (.amtDown 100)) = 5100 ∧
    (let q := ratio ccpiu_v2025 cpiu ccpiu_med_v2025 cpiu_med (.year 2017) 2026; adjustND 4150 q.1 q.2 (.amtDown 100)) = 5300 := by
  decide

/-! ## 2. Long-term-care limits: the index the IRS uses

Read literally, §213(d)(10)(B)(ii) compares the medical C-CPI-U (Dec 1999 = 100) with the medical CPI-U of
August 1996 times the ALL-ITEMS ratio C(2016)/P(2016); that gives far lower limits (generated files: e.g. $285 per
day for 2025 instead of $420). The published figures are reproduced exactly by splicing the medical CPI-U
(Aug 1996 → Aug 2016) onto the medical C-CPI-U (Aug 2016 → Aug of the preceding year) and rounding the amount to
the nearest $10. -/

def medSplice (M : Series) (cy base : Nat) : Nat :=
  let num := (cpiu_med.get? 2016 8).getD 0 * (M.get? (cy - 1) 8).getD 0
  let den := (cpiu_med.get? 1996 8).getD 0 * (M.get? 2016 8).getD 0
  roundNear (base * num) den 10

theorem ltc_2025_splice :
    [175, 200, 375, 750, 2000, 2500].map (medSplice ccpiu_med_v2024 2025) = [420, 480, 900, 1800, 4810, 6020] := by decide

theorem ltc_2026_splice :
    [175, 200, 375, 750, 2000, 2500].map (medSplice ccpiu_med_v2025 2026) = [430, 500, 930, 1860, 4960, 6200] := by decide

/-! ## 3. Estates and trusts: first bracket in 2026

§1(j)(3)(B)(i) (as amended by Pub. L. 119-21 §70101(b)) keeps base year 2017 for "the dollar amounts at which any
rate bracket higher than 12 percent ends and at which any rate bracket higher than 22 percent begins". The estate
table's first threshold is where the 24% bracket begins, so 2017 applies ($3,250); the published $3,300 is what
base year 2016 gives. -/
theorem est_first_bracket_2026 :
    RP25_32.br_est_1 = 3250 ∧
    adjust ccpiu_v2025 cpiu ccpiu_med_v2025 cpiu_med 2550 (.year 2016) 2026 (.incDown 50) = 3300 := by decide

/-! ## 4. MFS maximum 15-percent rate amount for 2025

§1(j)(5)(B)(ii)(I): "$479,000 (½ such amount in the case of a married individual filing a separate return)";
§1(j)(5)(C) indexes the $479,000. Half the indexed joint amount is $300,025; the published $300,000 is what
indexing a separate $239,500 gives. -/
theorem mfs_15_2025 :
    RP24_40.cg_f_mfj = 600050 ∧ RP24_40.cg_f_mfj / 2 = 300025 ∧
    adjust ccpiu_v2024 cpiu ccpiu_med_v2024 cpiu_med 239500 (.year 2017) 2025 (.incDown 50) = 300000 := by decide

/-! ## 5. Which C-CPI-U values the IRS used for base year 2024 (2026 items)

§1(f)(6)(A) requires the values as published at the August 2025 release, i.e. the REVISED 2024 values. Two
published 2026 figures distinguish the vintages, and both agree with the revised values. -/
theorem base2024_revised_values_used :
    -- §23(a)(4) $5,000: revised 2024 values give the published $5,120; the initial (Sept 2024) values give $5,110
    adjustND 5000 (ysum ccpiu_v2025 2025) (ysum ccpiu_v2025 2024) (.amtNear 10) = 5120 ∧
    adjustND 5000 (ysum ccpiu_v2025 2025) (ysum ccpiu_v2024 2024) (.amtNear 10) = 5110 ∧
    -- §6726(b)(2) $10,000,000: $10,230,000 published; initial values give $10,220,000
    adjustND 10000000 (ysum ccpiu_v2025 2025) (ysum ccpiu_v2025 2024) (.amtDown 10000) = 10230000 ∧
    adjustND 10000000 (ysum ccpiu_v2025 2025) (ysum ccpiu_v2024 2024) (.amtDown 10000) = 10220000 := by decide

/-! ## 6. The C-CPI-U average for 2026 cannot be formed

BLS published no October 2025 index (lapse in appropriations). The statutory 12-month C-CPI-U for 2026
(Sept 2025 – Aug 2026), which every 2027 adjustment and the literal reading of the penalty provisions for returns
filed in 2027 need, therefore has a missing month in the vintage of the August 2026 release. -/
theorem ccpiu_2026_incomplete : complete ccpiu_v2026 2026 = false ∧ ccpiu_v2026.get? 2025 10 = none := by decide

/-! ## 7. Qualifying-relative gross income limit for 2023 (§152(d)(1)(B))

Read literally, §151(d)(5)(A) makes "the exemption amount" zero after 2017, so the §152(d)(1)(B) test would be
"gross income less than $0". The IRS instead uses the §151(d)(1) $2,000 indexed from 1988 as if (d)(5)(A) did not
apply; that reproduces its 2024, 2025 and 2026 figures ($5,050, $5,200, $5,300). For 2023 the same method gives
$4,800; Rev. Proc. 2022-38 §3.24 publishes $4,700 — which is instead the §6334(d)(4)(C) / §642(b)(2)(C) figure
($4,150 from 2017, rounded as the IRS does there). -/
theorem qualifying_relative_2023 :
    RP22_38.exemption = 4800 ∧ RP23_34.exemption = 5050 ∧ RP24_40.exemption = 5200 ∧ RP25_32.exemption = 5300 ∧
    (let q := ratio ccpiu_v2022 cpiu ccpiu_med_v2024 cpiu_med (.year 2017) 2023; adjustND 4150 q.1 q.2 (.amtDown 100)) = 4700 := by
  decide

end TaxCode.Indexing.Findings
